text:
@Blade2uthat argument supports what you are calling socialism. 

I am not agreeing the DSA is bad. They are not socialist because America is a republic and unless the constitution is rewritten we are not socialist and can’t be. 

We can make the government represent the people.

@IanMartinez-Hay Fair, I shouldn't assume. But since you asked, the stuff that's actually moved rents is boring: legalize building more housing. Auckland upzoned about three-quarters of its residential land in 2016, got a construction boom, and peer-reviewed estimates put rents roughly 23–28% lower than they'd otherwise be. Austin made it easier to build in 2015, added ~120k units, and went from rents 15% above the national average to below it. Same reason Phoenix is cheaper than SoCal. Even Vienna, the go-to example for social housing, works because they build a ton of it. Rent control freezes the price for whoever already has the unit and does nothing about the shortage. You want more homes, not a rationing system for the ones we have.

My numbers might be off it's been a bit since I went over it it's been pretty consistently the same answer.

I'm all for decommoditizing housing in the long run but in the meantime we have to work inside the system we are in and this consistently works.

@JordanAF808it’s a lot of power to keep him in check, my main concern is that they don’t have a super majority to override his vetoes so actual lasting legislation (single payer healthcare, abortion rights, getting dark money out of politics, housing affordability) will need to wait. They can for sure stop the bleeding (especially by blocking any judge appointments), but the executive branch still has a lot (I think too much) power. 

Republicans are probably going to be a lot more amenable to impeaching Trump, especially if Dems can uncover more about the extent of the corruption and his approval sinks even lower. 

I want accountability and for this mess to be undone too. I just think we need to temper our expectations and understand that it won’t be a day one fix for a lot of the issues, and Dems will need to get creative to capitalize on their majorities.
